# AutoClicker for macOS

A powerful and elegant autoclicker application for macOS with support for both mouse clicks and keyboard key presses. Built with Python and native macOS frameworks.

![macOS](https://img.shields.io/badge/macOS-10.15+-blue.svg)
![Python](https://img.shields.io/badge/Python-3.9+-green.svg)
![License](https://img.shields.io/badge/license-MIT-orange.svg)

## Features

### Mouse Mode
- **Click Speed Control**: Configure clicks per second or per minute
- **Button Selection**: Choose between left, middle, or right mouse button
- **Smart Click**: Option to pause clicking when the mouse is moving
- **Customizable Delays**: Set a start delay and an auto-stop timer
- **Click at Current Position**: Clicks wherever your mouse cursor is located

### Keyboard Mode
- **Key Press Automation**: Automatically press any keyboard key
- **Custom Key Selection**: Pick any key by pressing it directly in the interface
- **Same Speed Controls**: Uses the same timing configuration as mouse mode

### General
- **Custom Hotkey**: Set any key combination to start/stop (default: F8)
- **Menu Bar Integration**: Lives in the macOS menu bar — no Dock icon
- **Dark Gradient UI**: Native-looking interface built with Cocoa
- **Countdown Timer**: Visual feedback when starting with a delay
- **Color-coded Status**: Green (ready), yellow (countdown), red (running), orange (paused)
- **Sleep/Wake Support**: Keyboard listener automatically restarts after system sleep

## Requirements

- macOS 10.15 (Catalina) or later
- Python 3.9 or higher
- PyObjC framework
- pynput library

## Installation

### Running from source

1. **Clone the repository**
   ```bash
   git clone https://github.com/Bitheos/autoclicker.git
   cd autoclicker
   ```

2. **Install dependencies**
   ```bash
   pip3 install pyobjc pynput
   ```

3. **Run the app**
   ```bash
   python3 src/autoclicker.py
   ```

### Building a standalone .app (no Python required)

Use the included build script — it compiles, signs, and cleans up permissions automatically:

```bash
chmod +x build.sh
./build.sh
```

The script will:
- Verify and install missing dependencies (PyInstaller, pyobjc, pynput)
- Compile with PyInstaller in `onedir` mode (required for Python 3.13 compatibility)
- Sign the app with an ad-hoc identity (no Apple Developer account needed)
- Remove the macOS quarantine flag
- Clean the Accessibility entry from the TCC database so permissions are requested fresh
- Optionally copy the app to `/Applications`

> **Note:** The app is not notarized. If macOS blocks it on first open, right-click → Open → Open anyway, or run:
> ```bash
> sudo xattr -rd com.apple.quarantine /Applications/Autoclicker.app
> ```

## Permissions

On first launch, macOS will automatically show the Accessibility permission dialog. Once you grant it:

- The **"Grant Accessibility permissions"** button disappears on its own
- The status label turns green and shows **Ready**
- The app is fully functional — no restart needed

The app detects the permission change in the background (polling every 2 seconds) without requiring any manual action beyond clicking Allow in System Preferences.

> If you rebuild the app, the build script clears the old TCC entry so permissions are always requested cleanly, avoiding the macOS bug where re-signed binaries silently lose their previously granted permissions.

Required permissions:

| Permission | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Accessibility | Simulate mouse clicks and keyboard presses |
| Input Monitoring | Detect global hotkey presses |

## Usage

1. Click the menu bar icon to open the panel
2. Configure your settings (speed, mode, delays, hotkey)
3. Press **Start** or your hotkey (default: **F8**) to begin
4. Press the hotkey again or click **Stop** to end the session

## Configuration

### Speed
- **Clicks/presses**: How many actions to perform (default: 20)
- **Per**: `second` or `minute`

### Delays
- **Start after**: Seconds to wait before the first action (0 = immediate)
- **Stop after**: Automatically stop after this many seconds (0 = run indefinitely)

### Hotkey
Click the hotkey button and press any key or combination (e.g. `F8`, `Ctrl+Shift+A`). Modifier keys alone are not accepted as a hotkey — at least one non-modifier key is required.

### Mouse Mode
- **Button**: Left, middle, or right
- **Smart click**: Pause automatically if the mouse has moved within the last N seconds

### Keyboard Mode
Click **Select key** and press the key you want to automate. The selected key is shown in the interface.

## Controls

| Action | How |
|---|---|
| Start / Stop | Hotkey (default F8) or Start/Stop button |
| Change hotkey | Click the hotkey button, press new keys |
| Select keyboard key | Click "Select key", press desired key |
| Quit | Click **Salir** in the panel |

## Troubleshooting

**App doesn't click or press keys**
- Grant Accessibility permission in System Preferences → Privacy & Security → Accessibility
- If the permission keeps disappearing after a rebuild, run `build.sh` again — it clears the stale TCC entry automatically

**Hotkey doesn't work**
- Check that Input Monitoring permission is granted
- Try a different hotkey
- Make sure no other app is capturing the same combination

**App blocked by macOS (Gatekeeper)**
- Right-click the app → Open → Open anyway
- Or: `sudo xattr -rd com.apple.quarantine /Applications/Autoclicker.app`

**`ModuleNotFoundError` during build**
- Do not exclude `xml`, `html`, `http`, or `urllib` from PyInstaller — pyobjc uses them internally
- Run `build.sh` which handles dependencies automatically

## Technical Details

- **PyObjC** — Python to Objective-C bridge for native macOS UI and system integration
- **Cocoa / AppKit** — NSPopover, NSStatusBar, NSTimer, NSSegmentedControl
- **Quartz / CGEvent** — Low-level mouse event simulation via `CGEventPost`
- **pynput** — Global keyboard listener running in a daemon thread
- **ctypes** — Direct calls to `AXIsProcessTrustedWithOptions` and `CFDictionaryCreate` without PyObjC (avoids segfault in PyInstaller bundles)
- **PyInstaller `onedir` mode** — Keeps all dependencies inside the `.app` bundle, preventing the Python 3.13 Team ID mismatch error when extracting to `/tmp`

